---
title: "Misc: Peer Presentations"
---

# Collated "Lessons Learned"

**1:**
- NEVER say your language isn't for programmers / is supposed to make programming easier!
- Keep slides on boring operations (e.g., standard arithmetic) short, don't read off a list of operations.
	* Basically, only talk about interesting stuff.
- Keep an eye on the time!

**2:**
- Professor will cook you if your example code can't be explained.
      - LLM code will be cooked! **Be fast on my feet!**
- **Make sure all code examples are explainable!!!!**

**3:**
- Have simple slides, have detailed notecards!
- Don't say anything I can't understand!
- A simple language explained well is superior to a complex language explained poorly.
- **End with a big example at the end!**
- Slides must be cohesively formatted!
      - Use consistent font and theming!
- Tie it back to the domain!

**4:**
- Don't fuck-up the start (design criteria).
- Focus on the actually-interesting parts of the language. 
- Have meaningful code comments!
- Be able to answer light questions on limitations.
      - If in doubt, **confidently make something up**.

# Detailed Notes

## 1. TableTop

> **Professor Rating: Best**

Problem Domain: Running a TTRPG is complicated.

Record Keeping Duties:
- Storylines
- Chars
- Encounters, World State
- XP
- Inventories
- so on and so forth.

"We want to simplify and streamline the organization of a game."

**Philosophy**: Simplicity
- Professor cringed hard at the line, "our users may not be programmers" (**DON'T SAY THIS!!! HE HAS SAID TO NOT SAY IT!!!**)

Type system is very C-like and standard.
- int, char, float, str, bool, strings

Cool stuff: Lists & (Hash)Maps
- Maps can't be arbitrarily constructed by users, this is used for game elements.

Game Elements are: Basic game structure defined via theses.
- e.g., WORLD STORY ENCOUNTER TASK ENEMY ENTITY etc.
- Declarative
- Control flow defined via attributes.
	* No loops! *(cool)*

**More on Attributes**: 
- AFFLICATION, UPON, REWARD, GIVE
- Within we have IF ELSE branches

Arithmetic ops are very standard.
- DIV / MULT / MOD / etc.
- On this slide, the professor just told them to skip. (**KEEP OUR SLIDE ON BORING OPS SHORT!**)

Subprograms: The interesting portion
- No return
- Arguments passed by reference.

They then showed a detailed code example and walked through it, before running out of time.

> **LESSONS LEARNED:**
> - NEVER say your language isn't for programmers / is supposed to make programming easier!
> - Keep slides on boring operations (e.g., standard arithmetic) short, don't read off a list of operations.
> 	* Basically, only talk about interesting stuff.
> - Keep an eye on the time!

**Professor's Rating**: "This was a very good presentation, the example was perfect, the slides were very informative."

## 2. BitCast

> **Professor Rating: Okay**

These guys ran through their Milestone document basically in chronological order.
- Update: Nevermind, they basically just went into their language's key structs (which were interesting) and explained them.

Four Key Structs:
- Zones: Design topology. Can be nested.
- Inventories: Map devices to names.
	* e.g., Two devices can be linked to the `@cloud`.
- Security Policies: Least priviledge and backups.
	* Even lets you set failure-modes at a high level *(have the door fail closed, have the thermostat fail open, etc.)*
- Behavior Policies: Constant, repeated, conditional checks.

They rushed through the remaining slides on type system and scope and such.

> **LESSONS LEARNED:**
> - Professor will cook you if your example code can't be explained.
>	- LLM code will be cooked! **Be fast on my feet!**
> - **Make sure all code examples are explainable!!!!**

**Professor's Rating**: "So, uh... I'm still bothered by the `Presence` thing. Because it's supposed to just be a-it's supposed to be the name of a device, and whatever function-right but is the device called Presence or is it a feature of your language? Seems like an object in the language or a library or class"

## 3. Crossline

> **Professor Rating: Poor**

Rationale: Work with email servers.

When going into Simplicity, Abstraction, Orthogonality, etc... **SAY LESS**
- You **will** fuck up explaining one of these, just focus on what the design favors! Group 1 literally just like went over 1 or 2 things, they did NOT go over all seven!

Their language is basically Java. I actually have no idea what their cool thing is?

They have way too much text on their slides.
- **SHOW LESS TEXT, SPEAK MORE**

> **LESSONS LEARNED:**
> - Have simple slides, have detailed notecards!
> - Don't say anything I can't understand!
> - A simple language explained well is superior to a complex language explained poorly.
> - **End with a big example at the end!**
> - Slides must be cohesively formatted!
>	- Use consistent font and theming!
> - Tie it back to the domain!

ASIDE: **CAN WE OVERRIDE TASK NAMES???** (OVERLOADING)

